---
/**
 * Redesign option — Time Management case study.
 *
 * Restructured from the live /work/time-management-system.astro into the
 * brief's Context / Complexity / Problem framing / Key decisions / System /
 * Craft / Collaboration / Outcome / Reflection shape. All facts, quotes and
 * assets below are pulled from the live page and existing public/images —
 * nothing here is invented.
 *
 * One flagged conflict: the brief for this redesign states the WFM rating
 * moved "2.9 to 3.7." The live case study's own evidence (Gartner MQ figure
 * + impact stat tiles) says 2.9 to 3.5. Rather than silently pick one, this
 * page uses 3.5 (the figure the live page's own evidence supports) and
 * calls the discrepancy out explicitly so it can be confirmed against the
 * real Gartner source before anything ships.
 *
 * Also preserved as-is, per the brief's instruction not to overclaim
 * causation: the rating lift is described as something Time Management was
 * "cited as a driver of," never as something the redesign caused outright.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseHero from "../../components/CaseHero.astro";
import DecisionBlock from "../../components/DecisionBlock.astro";
import ContentNeeded from "../../components/ContentNeeded.astro";

const IMG = "/images/work/time-management";
---

<Base title="Time Management | Kishore Sudhamalla">
  <CaseHero
    eyebrow="Case study 01 · Darwinbox"
    title="Time Management, rebuilt for the world."
    lede="Reimagining enterprise time management for global scale. An eight-year-old module used daily for attendance, leaves, timesheets, approvals, payroll dependencies and global compliance, with zero prior UX investment."
    meta={[
      { k: "Role", v: "Associate Director, UX" },
      { k: "Team", v: "3 designers · product · engineering" },
      { k: "Timeline", v: "2023 – 2024" },
      { k: "Platform", v: "Darwinbox HCM · 120+ countries" },
    ]}
    video={`${IMG}/tm-manager-dashboard`}
    poster={`${IMG}/tm-manager-dashboard-poster.jpg`}
    alt="Time Management manager dashboard"
    cards={[
      { eyebrow: "Gartner WFM score", value: "3.5", label: "Up from 2.9", note: "Darwinbox's Workforce Management score after the rebuild shipped. See the note on the 3.5 vs 3.7 discrepancy below." },
      { eyebrow: "Manager play rate", value: "73.1%", label: "Highest on the platform", note: "The dashboard view managers actually opened rather than the one they were given." },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="container-pg r-film" data-reveal>
    <p class="r-case-label">Film · with sound</p>
    <div class="r-film-frame">
      <video controls muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-hero-film-poster.jpg`}
        aria-label="A seventy second animated film of the Time Management redesign: the module before, one front door, the employee, manager and HRBP dashboards, one system for three roles, and the adoption numbers">
        <source src={`${IMG}/tm-hero-film.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
    </div>
    <p class="r-film-note">Seventy seconds. Press play and unmute. The dashboards are rebuilt from the shipped screens; names are demo data.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="container-pg r-case">
    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Context</p>
      <h2>An eight-year-old module with zero redesign and daily pain.</h2>
      <p>
        Time Management is one of Darwinbox's oldest and most heavily used modules. It covers attendance, leaves, timesheets, approvals, payroll dependencies and global compliance rules across 120+ countries. It had never had a dedicated redesign. By the time this project started it was the most complained-about module on the platform, used daily by over a million people.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Complexity -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Complexity</p>
      <h2>Why this was hard.</h2>
      <p>
        Employees, managers and HRBPs needed fundamentally different views of the same data, inside a system where attendance, leave and payroll calculations depend on each other and every customer's compliance configuration differs. Eight years of incremental feature requests had produced a module that was powerful and flexible but also dense, inconsistent and difficult to learn. The constraint was not to design something new. It was to rethink the module without breaking what existing customers depend on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem framing -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Problem framing</p>
      <h2>What we realized was actually wrong.</h2>
      <p>
        Primary research with TVS, Bigbasket and Licious, plus secondary
        analysis across Kronos, Workday, BambooHR and ZingHR, surfaced the same gap repeatedly. There was no central place for managers or employees to see time data at a glance. Everything lived behind transactional flows, so you had to already know what you were looking for. One enterprise manager put it directly:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        "If I had a magic wand to change one thing about the Time module,
        I'd add dashboards for employees and managers."
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The real problem was not missing features. The module had no front door: no single view that answered "what do I need to know or decide right now" for any of the three roles who depended on it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Key design decisions -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Key design decisions</p>
      <h2>Two decisions that shaped the system.</h2>

      <div class="r-decisions">
        <DecisionBlock
          number="1"
          problem="A dashboard needed to show primary time data alongside the context that makes it actionable (schedule, team comparison, exceptions) without splitting a manager's attention across the screen."
          options={[
            "3-column layout: primary data, team context and exceptions each in their own column.",
            "2-column layout: primary data with a single supporting context panel.",
          ]}
          decision="2-column layout: one primary view, one context panel."
          why="The 3-column version felt more flexible on paper, but fragmented attention. Managers had to scan three places to answer one question. The 2-column version gave every screen a clear hierarchy: one thing to look at first and one place to go for context."
          tradeoffs="Less can be visible at once than in the 3-column version. Some secondary data moved behind a panel toggle instead of staying always on screen."
          result="Shipped as the production information architecture for the manager and employee dashboards."
        />

        <DecisionBlock
          number="2"
          problem="Employees, managers and HRBPs each needed a different view of time data, but the existing module handled this by routing each role to different, inconsistent screens."
          options={[
            "Keep role-specific screens as separate surfaces, each evolving independently.",
            "Build one unified dashboard system, configured per role and permission set.",
          ]}
          decision="One unified dashboard framework adapted per persona, not three separate products."
          why="Separate screens compound inconsistency over time. Every future change has to be made three times and each role ends up learning a different mental model of the same data. A single framework meant one information model that could flex by role."
          tradeoffs="Higher upfront design and engineering cost to build a system that works across three permission levels at once, rather than shipping one role's view first and iterating."
          result="Employee, manager and HRBP dashboards all shipped from the same underlying system with zero module switching for any of the three roles."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- System -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">System</p>
      <h2>How it connects to the rest of the product.</h2>
      <p>
        Time Management sits at the intersection of attendance, leave and payroll. A change to how an exception is surfaced on the manager dashboard has downstream effects on payroll calculation and compliance reporting. The dashboards did not replace the underlying transactional flows (approvals, corrections, payroll sync). They sit on top of them as the layer that tells each role what to look at first, while the configuration-heavy flows underneath stayed intact for the customers who depend on their specific setup.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Craft -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Craft</p>
      <h2>The work.</h2>

      <div class="r-craft-compare">
        <figure>
          <img src={`${IMG}/before/old-attendance.webp`} alt="Attendance module before the redesign" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Before: attendance with no dashboard layer</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img src={`${IMG}/before/old-roster.webp`} alt="Roster view before the redesign" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Before: roster view</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="r-craft-grid">
        <figure>
          <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-employee-poster.jpg`}>
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-employee.webm`} type="video/webm" />
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-employee.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption>Employee dashboard, shipped</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-manager-poster.jpg`}>
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-manager.webm`} type="video/webm" />
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-manager.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption>Manager dashboard, shipped</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp-poster.jpg`}>
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp.webm`} type="video/webm" />
            <source src={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption>HRBP dashboard, shipped</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="r-craft-note">
        Mobile and responsive behaviour: the product is used globally across
        desktop and mobile by frontline and field employees.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="Mobile-specific screenshots and recordings were not carried over from the original case study in this pass. Pull the mobile views from the same source folder if they should be shown here." />
    </section>

    <!-- Collaboration -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Collaboration</p>
      <h2>How this got built.</h2>
      <p>
        Delivered with a team of three designers alongside product and engineering over roughly a year. Research and validation involved direct conversations with enterprise clients: TVS, Bigbasket and Licious in primary research, and later an in-market roadshow across Singapore, the Philippines and Thailand. We presented the shipped direction to Busy Bees, Singlife, Toyota, Security Bank, JG Summit, Cebu Pacific, BPI and Darwinbox's own SEA team.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Validation: the SEA roadshow, carried over from the original case study -->
    <section class="r-case-section r-wide" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Validation</p>
      <h2>Taken to 8 clients across Southeast Asia.</h2>
      <p>
        Before global rollout we ran a UX roadshow across Singapore, the Philippines and Thailand. We presented the designs to client teams and collected their reactions in the room.
      </p>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Who we presented to</p>
      <div class="r-roster">
        {[
          { name: "Busy Bees", industry: "Education", country: "Singapore" },
          { name: "Singlife", industry: "Banking & Financial Services", country: "Singapore" },
          { name: "Toyota", industry: "Manufacturing", country: "Philippines" },
          { name: "Security Bank", industry: "Banking & Financial Services", country: "Philippines" },
          { name: "JG Summit Holdings", industry: "Multi-industry", country: "Philippines" },
          { name: "Cebu Pacific", industry: "Airline & Transportation", country: "Philippines" },
          { name: "BPI", industry: "Banking & Financial Services", country: "Philippines" },
          { name: "Darwinbox", industry: "Internal teams", country: "Singapore & Philippines" },
        ].map((c) => (
          <div class="r-roster-item">
            <p class="r-roster-name">{c.name}</p>
            <p class="r-roster-industry">{c.industry}</p>
            <p class="r-roster-country">{c.country}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">On the ground</p>
      <div class="r-photos">
        {[
          { f: "session-studio-tiered", alt: "Presenting the redesigned time dashboard to a client team in a tiered studio space", wide: true },
          { f: "session-boardroom-dashboard", alt: "Walking a client team through the manager dashboard in a boardroom" },
          { f: "session-classroom", alt: "A larger roadshow session presented to client staff in a classroom setting" },
          { f: "session-manila-boardroom", alt: "A working session reviewing the designs with a client team" },
          { f: "busy-bees-team", alt: "The team at Busy Bees International after a roadshow session" },
          { f: "toyota-team", alt: "The team with client stakeholders at Toyota" },
          { f: "client-team-group", alt: "Group photo with a client team following a session" },
          { f: "client-session-group", alt: "Group photo with client stakeholders around the working table" },
        ].map((p) => (
          <figure class={`r-photo${p.wide ? " r-photo--wide" : ""}`}>
            <img src={`${IMG}/roadshow/${p.f}.webp`} alt={p.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">What they validated</p>
      <div class="r-validated">
        {[
          { t: "Consolidated view", q: "“Finally, everything in one place”" },
          { t: "Manager overview", q: "Team context at a glance" },
          { t: "Clock-in widget", q: "Placement felt natural" },
          { t: "Leave lineup", q: "Who's out, who's in" },
        ].map((v) => (
          <div class="r-validated-item">
            <p class="r-validated-t">{v.t}</p>
            <p class="r-validated-q">{v.q}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Post-release review with clients</p>
      <div class="r-review">
        <div>
          <p class="r-review-head">What went well</p>
          <ul>
            <li>All functionality aligned with user intent</li>
            <li>Consolidated view of leave and attendance</li>
            <li>Manager overview of all their reports</li>
            <li>Evident clock-in widget to address missed ones</li>
            <li>Leave lineup identified as a standout feature</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Now everything's in one place, and we look forward to enabling this.” <span>Toyota</span></p>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Time module is more detailed than before.” <span>Shakey's</span></p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="r-review-head">Areas of improvement / asks</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Dashboard utility with just leave data when attendance is not enabled</li>
            <li>Offline capability in mobile: leave application for desk-less employees with limited connectivity</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Time dashboard with only leave data, what would it look like?” <span>Busy Bees</span></p>
          <p class="r-review-note">Most clients did not have the employee-shifts use case. Those that did asked for a separate deep-dive call on scheduler and timesheets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Outcome -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Outcome</p>
      <h2>What shipped and what we know about its use.</h2>

      <div class="r-stat-row">
        <div><p class="r-stat-v">1.16M</p><p class="r-stat-l">Dashboard plays, employee + manager</p></div>
        <div><p class="r-stat-v">73.1%</p><p class="r-stat-l">Highest manager play rate on the platform</p></div>
        <div><p class="r-stat-v">3.5</p><p class="r-stat-l">Gartner WFM score (up from 2.9)</p></div>
      </div>

      <table class="r-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Dashboard</th><th>Plays</th><th>Play rate</th><th>Unique visitors</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Employee Time Dashboard</td><td>116,374</td><td>71.6%</td><td>147,357</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Manager Time Dashboard</td><td>28,547</td><td>73.1%</td><td>34,693</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="r-table-caption">Adoption in the first weeks after launch.</p>

      <p>
        Darwinbox's Gartner Magic Quadrant Workforce Management score moved
        from 2.9 to 3.5 between 2021 and 2024 and the company moved into Challenger status over that period. Time Management was cited as a contributing factor. The evidence here does not support saying the redesign alone caused the score to move, only that it was named as part of the reason.
      </p>

      <ContentNeeded note="The brief for this redesign states the rating moved from 2.9 to 3.7. The source case study's own evidence (Gartner MQ figure, impact stat tiles) supports 2.9 to 3.5. Confirm which figure is current against the actual Gartner report before publishing. This page uses 3.5 pending that confirmation." />

      <blockquote>
        "Really great job on time management, excellent UI and features. The
        main highlight for me is how it simplified direct-reportee
        attendance insight, analysis, comparison with the org, and the
        schedule planner."
        <cite>Monik Suthar, Darwinbox customer community</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Reflection</p>
      <h2>What I'd do differently.</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest lesson was committing to the data model earlier. We
        spent too long exploring the 3-column layout because it felt more
        flexible, when the evidence for a simpler 2-column hierarchy was already there. The SEA roadshow was the most direct and highest-quality validation in the whole project, yet it happened near-final rather than at wireframe stage. By then good feedback could only go into the backlog instead of shaping core decisions that were already locked in. Validating earlier, even with rougher artifacts, would have let that feedback change the design.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .r-film { padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem) 0; }
  .r-film-frame { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 1.25rem; overflow: hidden; background: #000; max-width: 72rem; }
  .r-film-frame video { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .r-film-note { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0.75rem 0 0; }
  .r-case { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem); }
  .r-case-section > :is(p, h2, h3, blockquote, ul, ol, .r-case-label, .cn, .r-decisions, .r-sub-label) { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .r-case-section { margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .r-case-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-case-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 2.6vw, 1.875rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section p {
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section blockquote {
    margin: 1.5rem 0; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-ink-3);
    font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic; color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .r-case-section blockquote cite {
    display: block; margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.8125rem; font-style: normal; color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .r-decisions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .r-craft-compare, .r-craft-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .r-craft-compare { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
  .r-craft-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .r-craft-compare figure, .r-craft-grid figure { margin: 0; }
  .r-craft-compare img, .r-craft-grid video {
    width: 100%; border-radius: 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    display: block; background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-craft-compare figcaption, .r-craft-grid figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }
  .r-craft-note { margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }

  .r-stat-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .r-stat-v {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .r-stat-l { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0.25rem 0 0; }

  .r-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem; font-size: 0.875rem; }
  .r-table th, .r-table td {
    text-align: left; padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .r-table th { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3); }
  .r-table td:first-child { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .r-table-caption { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .r-craft-compare, .r-craft-grid, .r-stat-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  /* Validation (roadshow) */
  .r-sub-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem !important;
  }
  .r-roster {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 1px;
    background: var(--color-border); border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 1rem; overflow: hidden;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) { .r-roster { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); } }
  .r-roster-item { background: var(--color-bg); padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem; }
  .r-roster-item p { margin: 0 !important; }
  .r-roster-name { font-size: 0.9375rem !important; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink) !important; line-height: 1.3 !important; }
  .r-roster-industry { font-size: 0.75rem !important; line-height: 1.45 !important; margin-top: 0.2rem !important; }
  .r-roster-country {
    font-size: 0.6875rem !important; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3) !important; margin-top: 0.5rem !important; line-height: 1.3 !important;
  }

  .r-photos { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 0.75rem; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .r-photos { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 1rem; } }
  .r-photo {
    margin: 0; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 0.875rem; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-photo img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; display: block; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .r-photo--wide { grid-column: span 2; grid-row: span 2; aspect-ratio: auto; } }

  .r-validated { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 0.75rem; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .r-validated { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); } }
  .r-validated-item {
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 0.875rem; background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-validated-item p { margin: 0 !important; }
  .r-validated-t { font-size: 0.875rem !important; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink) !important; line-height: 1.3 !important; }
  .r-validated-q { font-size: 0.75rem !important; font-style: italic; color: var(--color-ink-3) !important; margin-top: 0.3rem !important; line-height: 1.4 !important; }

  .r-review { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2rem; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .r-review { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; } }
  .r-review-head { font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink) !important; margin: 0 0 0.75rem !important; font-size: 0.9375rem !important; }
  .r-review ul { margin: 0 0 1.25rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .r-review li {
    font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink-2);
    padding: 0.55rem 0 0.55rem 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); position: relative;
